      <h1>I-130 Processing Time: Marriage Green Card Timeline</h1>
      <div class="entry-content">
<p>There is no single I-130 processing time. For a spouse of a U.S. citizen living in the United States, the full marriage green card process has recently run about one to two years. Families going through consular processing abroad usually wait longer, because the National Visa Center and the embassy add their own stages. Your country, service center and paperwork decide the rest.</p>
<p>The I-130 processing time is only one link in that chain. It gets the attention because it's the first thing you file and the first thing you can measure. Couples who plan around the petition alone are usually surprised by what comes after it.</p>
<p>Our San Francisco office handles family petitions through <a href="/immigration-lawyer-san-francisco/">our immigration practice</a>.</p>
<h2 id="two-paths-and-why-they-diverge">Two Paths, and Why They Diverge</h2>
<p>Every marriage case starts with the same form. Form I-130, Petition for Alien Relative, is filed by the U.S. citizen or green card holder (the petitioner) for the spouse (the beneficiary). The petition does not change based on where your spouse lives. Everything after approval does.</p>
<p><strong>Adjustment of status.</strong> Your spouse is already in the United States and files Form I-485 to become a permanent resident without leaving. If you are a U.S. citizen, an immigrant visa number is always available to your spouse, so the two forms can often be filed together. That is concurrent filing.</p>
<p><strong>Consular processing.</strong> Your spouse is abroad. The approved petition goes to the Department of State's National Visa Center, then to a U.S. embassy or consulate. Your spouse files the visa application, submits civil documents, sees a panel physician, and interviews overseas.</p>
<p>The divergence is not the petition. It is the queue behind it.</p>
<p>So which path is faster? Neither, automatically. Staying inside the United States is more comfortable. You skip the embassy, and your spouse can generally apply for work authorization while waiting. But adjustment usually ends in a USCIS interview where an officer can question both of you. Consular cases end at a window, with an officer who has limited time and wide discretion. Neither is risk-free.</p>
<p>One more variable matters. A spouse of a U.S. citizen is an "immediate relative," so no visa number is needed. A spouse of a lawful permanent resident falls into a preference category instead, so the Visa Bulletin decides when a number is available. That category has been current for long stretches in recent years and has also retrogressed, so check the current bulletin before you plan anything. When a green card holder naturalizes, the petition can be upgraded, which is usually worth doing.</p>
<h2 id="the-full-timeline-stage-by-stage">The Full Timeline, Stage by Stage</h2>
<p>Here is the sequence, who controls each stage, and what usually goes wrong.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Who handles it</th><th>What usually causes delay</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I-130 filed</td><td>Petitioner files with USCIS</td><td>Wrong lockbox, unsigned form, incorrect fee, no Form I-130A</td></tr><tr><td>Receipt and review</td><td>USCIS service center</td><td>Case transfers between centers, heavy queue at the assigned office</td></tr><tr><td>I-130 decision</td><td>USCIS</td><td>Request for Evidence, unresolved prior marriages, name or date inconsistencies</td></tr><tr><td>I-485 (in the U.S.) or NVC and embassy (abroad)</td><td>USCIS, or the National Visa Center and the consulate</td><td>Missing civil documents, slow police certificates, visa number limits</td></tr><tr><td>Interview</td><td>USCIS field office, or the consulate abroad</td><td>Scheduling backlog, rescheduling, disorganized relationship evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Decision</td><td>USCIS or the consular officer</td><td>Post-interview review, extra document requests, administrative processing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Two notes on reading that table. The stages do not overlap the way people assume. A petition can be approved quickly and then sit at the next stage, which is why "my I-130 was approved fast" tells you little about the total wait. And the only estimate worth trusting is the USCIS processing-times tool, which reports by form type and office. Check it the week you file and again after any transfer notice.</p>
<p>Interviews are where preparation shows. A consular interview is short and blunt. A USCIS adjustment interview runs longer and turns on small inconsistencies: an address history with a gap, a joint account opened the week before filing, a birth date that differs by a digit between two documents. <a href="/blog/what-happens-during-a-uscis-interview/">What happens during a USCIS interview</a> covers the format.</p>
<h2 id="why-i-130-processing-times-vary-so-much">Why I-130 Processing Times Vary So Much</h2>
<p>USCIS does not adjudicate petitions in one place. Cases are routed by the form you file and the petitioner's address, some centers carry heavier loads than others, and USCIS moves cases between centers. A transfer changes your expectations without changing your receipt date.</p>
<p>Four things move the number. Case type: an immediate relative spouse is a different workload than a preference spouse. Country: it matters less at the petition stage and far more at the consulate. File quality: a clean file with certified translations moves, and a file that draws an RFE gets parked. Volume: filing surges push later cases behind more of them.</p>
<p>One warning about averages. An average hides the tail. If the average is a year and your case lands in the slowest tenth, you planned around a number that never applied to you. Look at the published range, not the headline figure a blog post pulled from it three years ago.</p>
<h2 id="what-actually-slows-a-case-down">What Actually Slows a Case Down</h2>
<p>Most delays are administrative, not legal. That's good news. Most administrative problems can be fixed before you file.</p>
<ul><li><strong>A Request for Evidence.</strong> USCIS pauses the file, you get a deadline, and the case sits until someone picks it up again. Even a fast, complete response costs time.</li><li><strong>Civil documents that are hard to get.</strong> Police certificates from some jurisdictions, birth certificates from slow registries, records scattered by a move or a conflict.</li><li><strong>Name and date mismatches.</strong> Transliteration differences, maiden names, a passport spelling that does not match the marriage certificate. This is the most common fixable defect in family petitions.</li><li><strong>Visa number limits.</strong> In preference cases the Visa Bulletin can move backward. Retrogression is not an error in your file. It's a queue that got longer.</li><li><strong>Administrative processing.</strong> A consular officer can send a case into extra review after the interview, with no set end date.</li><li><strong>Uncertified translations.</strong> USCIS wants a full English translation plus the translator's signed certification that it is complete and accurate and that they are competent to translate. Partial translations get rejected.</li><li><strong>The path box on the petition.</strong> Part 4 of Form I-130 asks whether your spouse will adjust status inside the United States or process abroad. You pick one. Pick wrong, or leave it blank, and USCIS decides for you after approval, which can send a case that belonged at a field office to the National Visa Center instead.</li></ul>
<p>That single checkbox explains a surprising number of "approved, then stuck" cases. If your plans change after filing, you tell USCIS while the petition is pending, and you file Form I-824 if it has already been approved and moved.</p>
<h2 id="documents-to-gather-before-you-file">Documents to Gather Before You File</h2>
<p>Pull the whole file together first, then file.</p>
<ol><li>Proof of the petitioner's citizenship or status: passport, birth certificate, naturalization certificate, or both sides of a green card.</li><li>The marriage certificate, plus divorce decrees or death certificates showing any earlier marriage ended for both spouses.</li><li>Evidence the marriage is real: joint lease, joint accounts, shared insurance, joint tax returns, birth certificates for children you share.</li><li>Affidavits from people with direct personal knowledge of the marriage. Each one must include the affiant's full name and address, their date and place of birth, and how they know the marriage. Treat affidavits as support for documents, not a substitute for them.</li><li>Passport-style photos, where the instructions ask for them.</li><li>Form I-130A, completed by your spouse.</li><li>Certified English translations of anything not in English.</li><li>A copy of everything you send.</li></ol>
<p>The authoritative checklist is on the <a href="https://www.uscis.gov/i-130">USCIS I-130 page</a>, and it is worth reading twice. USCIS is also clear about what not to send: no originals unless the instructions ask, nothing with a battery or a chip, and no intimate photos. They do not help.</p>
<h2 id="what-the-case-costs">What the Case Costs</h2>
<p>Budget in four buckets: government filing fees, the medical exam, translation and copying, and travel if the interview is abroad.</p>
<p>USCIS sets the petition fee and, in an adjustment case, the I-485 fee. Both change from time to time, and a wrong fee gets the package rejected and mailed back, so confirm the amount on the USCIS fee schedule first. A consular case usually skips the I-485 fee but adds a panel physician exam overseas and travel to the consulate. A consular case also carries a separate immigrant visa fee at the National Visa Center stage.</p>
<p>Plan for the whole case at the start. The later payments tend to arrive when you're least ready.</p>
<h2 id="if-the-i-130-is-denied">If the I-130 Is Denied</h2>
<p>An I-130 denial is a decision about the relationship. It isn't a deportation order and not the end of the case.</p>
<p>If an I-485 was pending alongside it, that application generally falls with the petition, and the person's status situation can change quickly. That is the part to take seriously.</p>
<p>The notice states the reason, and the reason drives the response. If USCIS found the evidence too thin, refiling with a stronger record is sometimes cleanest. If the denial rests on a legal conclusion, a motion to reopen or reconsider, or an appeal, may fit instead. Deadlines are short, and a missed one closes doors that were open a week earlier.</p>
<p>Some denials are avoidable: a missing decree from a prior marriage, a spouse who filed under a different name, a petitioner who did not qualify to file. <a href="/blog/what-can-cause-a-family-based-immigration-application-to-be-denied/">What can cause a family-based application to be denied</a> covers the usual triggers.</p>
<p>One distinction to keep straight. A denied petition is not the same as an approved petition followed by a refused visa at the consulate. The second involves the officer's findings on admissibility and may carry waiver options.</p>
<h2 id="where-to-go-from-here">Where to Go From Here</h2>
<p>Timelines are estimates. Your case is a file with your name on it, and the only reliable way to know where it stands is to read every notice and track the receipt number.</p>
<p>If you are about to file, or you filed a while ago and the case has gone quiet, Sulfab Law Office reviews family petitions from our office at 369 Pine St in the Financial District. Call (415) 360-5266, or <a href="/contact/">request a case review</a>.</p>
<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3 id="how-long-does-an-i-130-take-for-a-spouse-of-a-u-s-citizen">How long does an I-130 take for a spouse of a U.S. citizen?</h3>
<p>There is no fixed answer. In recent years spouse petitions have commonly taken around a year at many service centers, and plenty take longer. The spread depends on the service center and how clean the filing is. The USCIS processing-times tool shows current ranges by office, so check it rather than trusting a single figure online.</p>
<h3 id="can-i-file-form-i-130-and-form-i-485-at-the-same-time">Can I file Form I-130 and Form I-485 at the same time?</h3>
<p>Yes, if your spouse is in the United States, entered legally, and an immigrant visa number is available. For the spouse of a U.S. citizen a number is always available, so concurrent filing is common. A visa overstay is generally forgiven for the spouse of a U.S. citizen. An entry without inspection usually is not. For the spouse of a green card holder, you generally wait for a number under the Visa Bulletin first.</p>
<h3 id="does-an-approved-i-130-give-my-spouse-a-green-card">Does an approved I-130 give my spouse a green card?</h3>
<p>No. Approval means USCIS accepts that the qualifying relationship exists. It does not grant status by itself. Your spouse still needs an approved adjustment application or an immigrant visa issued abroad. USCIS says this on the form page, and it surprises people who assumed approval was the finish line.</p>
<h3 id="which-is-faster-adjustment-of-status-or-consular-processing">Which is faster, adjustment of status or consular processing?</h3>
<p>Neither is automatically faster. Adjustment skips the National Visa Center and embassy scheduling, but it adds a USCIS field office interview and that office's backlog. Consular timing depends heavily on the specific post. Compare posted times for your city and your spouse's country rather than a general rule.</p>
<h3 id="can-my-spouse-work-or-travel-while-the-case-is-pending">Can my spouse work or travel while the case is pending?</h3>
<p>In an adjustment case, your spouse can generally apply for a work permit and a travel document while the case is pending. Travel before that document is approved can create real problems, so do not book anything without checking first. Consular cases carry no interim benefits, because your spouse is outside the country.</p>
<h3 id="what-happens-if-we-miss-a-request-for-evidence-deadline">What happens if we miss a Request for Evidence deadline?</h3>
<p>USCIS can deny the petition. The notice sets a deadline and a mailing address, and the deadline is not flexible in an ordinary case. If a document genuinely cannot be obtained in time, respond with what you have and explain the gap in writing. Silence is the worst option. If the date has passed, get advice quickly.</p>
<h3 id="how-much-does-a-marriage-green-card-cost">How much does a marriage green card cost?</h3>
<p>There is no single figure, and anyone quoting one is guessing. Budget four buckets: government filing fees, the medical exam, translation and copying, and travel if the interview is abroad. A consular case adds an immigrant visa fee at the National Visa Center stage. Fees change, so confirm the current amount on the USCIS fee schedule before you file.</p>
